In	tournament-based	crowdsourcing,	many	solutions	from	solvers	compete	for	a	prize
that	the	winner(s)	collects.	Examples	include	grand	challenges	such	as	the	Longitude
Prize,	as	well	as	non-grand	challenges	such	as	the	Goldcorp	challenge	that	helped
Goldcorp	better	pinpoint	gold	on	its	property.
As	a	process,	crowdsourcing	has	four	major	steps:
delineation,	prize	setting,	and	broadcast	of	the	problem;
self-selection	of	potential	solvers	to	solve	the	problem;
solution	evaluation;
reception	and	implementation	of	solution	by	seeker.
The	advantages	of	crowdsourcing	include:
access	to	a	talent	pool	with	knowledge	that	has	more	depth	and	breadth;
higher	probability	of	obtaining	a	higher-value	solution;
opportunity	to	signal	strategy	to	coopetitors;
obtaining	solutions	to	problems	that	only	crowds	can	solve;
access	to	a	solution	that	already	exists	somewhere	and	not	having	to	reinvent	the
wheel;
freeing	up	management	resources	to	work	on	something	else.
