A	key	character	in	globalization	is	the	multinational	corporation	(MNC).	This	is	a
firm	that	has	established	product-market-positions	and/or	capabilities	in	at	least	two
countries.	A	firm	is	a	position	multinational	if	it	designs,	develops,	and	produces	its
products	 at	 home,	 but	 sells	 them	 in	 two	 or	 more	 countries.	 If	 a	 firm’s	 design,
development,	and	production	of	a	product	are	done	in	many	countries	but	the	product
is	sold	in	only	one	country,	the	firm	is	said	to	be	a	capabilities	multinational.	If	a	firm
has	market	positions	in	two	or	more	countries	and	the	capabilities	that	it	needs	come
from	two	or	more	countries,	the	firm	is	classified	as	a	global	multinational.
The	extent	to	which	globalization	takes	place	in	an	industry	is	a	function	of:
technological	innovation;
consumer	tastes	and	needs;
government	policies;
multinationals’	strategies.
Some	reasons	for	firms	becoming	multinationals	include:
the	search	for	growth;
opportunity	to	stabilize	earnings;

